# shell: desktop
# home: fixture
# prefs: factory
# setup: trail
# board: only
# What the shell was doing, kept so that it can be asked afterwards.
#
# Every failure this shell has had was diagnosed by adding a print and
# running it again, which is not a thing a person using it can do.  So
# the shell keeps a ring of what it did -- always on, never written down
# until something goes wrong -- and here it is simply asked for.  What
# is asserted is a SEQUENCE: the ask arriving, the run beginning, and
# the states it went through, in the order they happened.
wait 60
note the ports the shell asked at startup, and what answered
expect-read trail:mount /devices/tiku
click 980 30
wait 10
menu-pick Programming
wait 10
menu-pick TikuBasic
wait 250
expect-title Project
note starting an application is one of the things it keeps, by name
expect-read trail:launch TikuBasic
click 71 8
wait 8
menu-pick Open Project…
wait 40
click 400 220
wait 6
click 675 413
wait 60
click-window Project
wait 200
note opening a project looks for boards, and the look is a line a port
expect-read trail:look done
click 175 8
wait 8
menu-pick tiku
wait 10
key Down
wait 8
click 71 8
wait 8
menu-pick Run blink.bas on tiku
note the ask reaching the shell over the wire, named by what it was
wait 60
expect-read trail:wire brun
note and the run itself, from where it began to where it ended
expect-read trail:run start on tiku
wait 500
expect-read trail:run ended
quit
